axis: tie-break-basis
axis_gloss: "when two candidates are otherwise equal, whether the cheaper-to-answer one wins or the one the actor explicitly chose this turn"
poles:
  a: lower-effort-first
  b: explicit-choice-first
split: genuinely-split
draft_status: axis-only
leverage: 12
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(8) x split_weight(1) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.5)"

question: >
  On tie break basis — when two candidates are otherwise equal, whether the
  cheaper-to-answer one wins or the one the actor explicitly chose this turn —
  the records assert both lower effort first (3 judgments, 2 authored) and
  explicit choice first (5 judgments, 1 authored). Which should be the stated
  rule, and where is the exception?

why_it_matters: >
  Until this is answered, a record below Dave inherits whichever side it happens
  to copy. Full draft pending: the proposal batch for this axis did not
  complete; the axis, poles, counts and evidence are real, the options below are
  the generic form.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    3 judgments sit at lower-effort-first (2 authored) and 5 at
    explicit-choice-first (1 authored). The repository asserts both directions on
    this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    The full proposal.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      lower effort first becomes the stated rule across its scope.
    cost: "not yet drafted"
    risk: "not yet drafted"
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "not yet drafted"
    evidence_for:
      - "J0250"
      - "J1023"
      - "J1200"
  - name: B
    description: >
      explicit choice first becomes the stated rule across its scope.
    cost: "not yet drafted"
    risk: "not yet drafted"
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "not yet drafted"
    evidence_for:
